00:00:01.612 --> 00:00:03.070
QINGYANG CHEN:
Welcome to our talk.

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:05.195
We're going to be talking
about building containers

00:00:05.195 --> 00:00:08.410
faster with Jib, which is a
container image builder we

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:11.244
built for Java applications.

00:00:11.244 --> 00:00:12.410
APPU GOUNDAN: So who are we?

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:14.440
We're a team based
out of New York.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:18.150
We build tools for Java
developers targeting the cloud.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:18.880
I'm Appu.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:24.090
QINGYANG CHEN: And I'm Q. So
cloud deployments nowadays are

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.760
moving from VMs to containers.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.640
Many of you are probably already
familiar with containers,

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.935
which allow you
to, say, split up

00:00:31.935 --> 00:00:34.230
your application
into micro-services

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:37.950
and orchestrate these with
tools such as Kubernetes.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:41.400
And Java developers are already
familiar with building jars.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.030
Containers are sort
of the same concept.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:46.980
The idea behind Java is
very similar to the idea

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.610
behind containers, which is
this idea of write once, run

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:51.790
anywhere.

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:54.690
However, building containers
for Java applications

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:59.160
can be quite a confusing
and tricky process.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:02.110
APPU GOUNDAN: So let's go
through an example of a Java

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:04.120
developer who is
new to containers

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.850
and trying to figure out
how to write a docker file

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:08.249
to build a container.

00:01:08.249 --> 00:01:09.290
QINGYANG CHEN: All right.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:11.410
So let's say I'm
a Java developer

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:14.380
and I'm building a
website for a pet clinic.

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:17.470
I want to containerize my
back end-- my Java back end.

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:20.200
And I want my container
image to be on my registry

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.960
at ilovejava.io/petclinic-app.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:25.150
So what do I do first?

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:28.570
I go and I search build
Java docker image.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:31.960
And I read the top
few tutorials and I'm

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:36.680
able to write this docker file.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:40.220
So I run Docker Build, but
it takes a very long time

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.707
to actually do the build.

00:01:42.707 --> 00:01:45.290
APPU GOUNDAN: So some of you may
have already written a docker

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:48.120
file like this, but to
see what's going on here,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.150
you're choosing a
base image, you're

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:51.890
installing a bunch of things--

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:54.380
Python software properties,
some other stuff

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.600
I don't really understand.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:58.700
You have to install Java 8.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.080
You have to accept
the Oracle license.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.320
Then you run Install Maven.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.180
You put your application
on your container

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.690
and then you do your
build on your container.

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:10.110
It's a lot going on.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:12.530
There might be a way to
simplify this and remove

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:14.750
all the unnecessary runs.

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:15.500
QINGYANG CHEN: OK.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.990
So I read some more
tutorials and I'm

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:24.280
able to write this much
better docker file.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:27.700
I'm using a well-maintained
base image-- the openjdk base

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:29.500
image off of Docker Hub.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.680
And all I'm doing is I'm copying
my fat jar into my container

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:36.530
image and setting my entry
point to run that fat jar.

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:37.590
It's a lot simpler--

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.560
a lot fewer layers.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:41.045
APPU GOUNDAN: So this is nice.

00:02:41.045 --> 00:02:42.670
It's very obvious
what's going on here.

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:44.740
I understand all the lines.

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:46.390
However, when I
ran this build, I

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:49.770
saw that my image was
over 300 megabytes.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:53.180
And I know some people are
concerned about disk space.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:58.110
So maybe there's a way
to make this smaller.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.780
QINGYANG CHEN: So OK.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:03.070
So I do some research and I
find that the openjdk base

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:07.825
image is 284 megabytes by
itself, and that's huge,

00:03:07.825 --> 00:03:10.720
making my entire application
over 300 megabytes.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.720
So I do some more
searching and I

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:18.520
find that I can actually use
the Alpine version of openjdk.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:21.340
Alpine is a tiny
Linux distribution,

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:23.800
which, by itself, is
only about 4 megabytes,

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.140
and most only just
using a JRE version.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:28.870
So this base image
is only 82 megabytes,

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:30.610
making my entire
image a lot smaller

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:33.100
than what I started with.

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:35.010
However, it seems that
when I run the build,

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.597
it still takes a
while to do the build.

00:03:37.597 --> 00:03:39.430
APPU GOUNDAN: So it's
not obvious from here,

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:41.530
but when you do a docker
build, the first step

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:44.115
is sending the context
over to the docker daemon.

00:03:44.115 --> 00:03:45.490
And the context
is everything you

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:48.461
need to build your container.

00:03:48.461 --> 00:03:50.710
By default, the context is
everything in the directory

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:52.140
with your docker file.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:55.110
But in here, you see we only
need our pet clinic fat jar.

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:58.410
So maybe there is a way
we can limit what we send.

00:03:58.410 --> 00:03:59.160
QINGYANG CHEN: OK.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:00.326
So I do some more searching.

00:04:00.326 --> 00:04:04.330
I find that there is a
docker file best practices,

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:06.850
and that's a lot
of best practices.

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:10.010
I see in there there's something
about a docker ignore file.

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:13.000
So I do some research
on docker ignore files

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.280
and I'm able to write
this docker ignore file.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.620
It ignores everything
in my project directory

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:21.160
except for the fat jar that I
want to copy into my container

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.160
image.

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:25.360
So now, my build
runs a lot faster,

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:29.165
so I can now do docker push and
how my image be on registry.

00:04:29.165 --> 00:04:32.920
But it seems that even if I just
change a single line of code,

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:34.900
my push still takes a while.

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:36.470
Why is that?

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:39.250
APPU GOUNDAN: So usually,
every line in your docker file

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:41.765
creates a new layer and
your image is made up

00:04:41.765 --> 00:04:43.030
of a bunch of layers.

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:44.440
In our example,
one of our layers

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:45.880
is our whole application.

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:48.130
So if you change a tiny part
of your application, when

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:49.960
you build, you have to
create that layer again.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:51.010
You have to send it over.

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:53.450
If your application
is pretty large,

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:54.916
then that might take a while.

00:04:54.916 --> 00:04:57.040
Perhaps there is a way we
can split our application

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.480
into smaller layers.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:04:59.230
QINGYANG CHEN: OK.

00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:01.810
So I do some more
tutorials and I'm

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:04.480
able to write this
better docker file.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.820
I split up my application
to two layers--

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:10.570
one for my dependency jars
and one for my classes files.

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:12.880
This way, when I only
change my classes files,

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:16.120
the only layer that I need to
resend when I do a docker push

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:20.090
is my classes layer, and not
my usually much larger layer

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:22.000
with my dependency jars.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.172
So I think I'm
good to go, right?

00:05:24.172 --> 00:05:25.630
APPU GOUNDAN: Yeah,
except it looks

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:27.129
like we still need
to run some Maven

00:05:27.129 --> 00:05:29.704
goals to prepare for our build.

00:05:29.704 --> 00:05:31.120
That's the problem
with having two

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:33.700
build files is you have to
maintain two build files.

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:35.830
Maybe there's a way we
can do this in Maven.

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:36.580
QINGYANG CHEN: OK.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:38.050
So I do some more
searching and I

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:40.360
find that there are
actually Maven plug plugins

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:44.590
that I can use to define my
docker build within my Maven

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:45.880
Palm XML.

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:48.630
So I delete my four
lines of docker file,

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:50.770
add just 20 lines of
Maven configuration.

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:53.200
I'll do my entire
build from Maven.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:53.890
Great.

00:05:53.890 --> 00:05:57.260
I think I'm good to go.

00:05:57.260 --> 00:05:59.360
APPU GOUNDAN: So there
was a lot going on here.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:02.450
We wrote a docker file that
