your jar it up into a jar.

00:11:55.070 --> 00:11:57.110
And with Jib, you
take your application

00:11:57.110 --> 00:12:00.620
and you Jib it up
into a container.

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:05.320
So let's go into a little
bit of how Jib works.

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:07.630
QINGYANG CHEN: So we
kept a few goals in mind

00:12:07.630 --> 00:12:09.340
when we were developing Jib.

00:12:09.340 --> 00:12:12.260
First, we wanted to have
Jib be purely in Java,

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:16.240
so that you can containerize
your Java project with nothing

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:19.450
but just your Maven or
Gradle build systems.

00:12:19.450 --> 00:12:22.810
We wanted Jib to be
optimized for Java occasions

00:12:22.810 --> 00:12:26.230
so that you can deploy your
changes fast and incrementally.

00:12:26.230 --> 00:12:30.610
And we wanted Jib to be able
to build images reproducibly.

00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:32.950
So we implemented Jib
completely in Java

00:12:32.950 --> 00:12:34.920
so that you can build
with just your Maven

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:38.320
or Gradle build systems,
and the benefits of these

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:41.330
are that you don't need to
install docker or have docker

00:12:41.330 --> 00:12:42.400
CLI.

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:44.470
You don't need to run
the docker daemon.

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:46.834
You don't need to have root
in order to run that daemon,

00:12:46.834 --> 00:12:48.625
and you're not actually
running a container

00:12:48.625 --> 00:12:51.070
in order to build a container.

00:12:51.070 --> 00:12:52.822
So how do we achieve this?

00:12:52.822 --> 00:12:54.280
Well, first, it
helps to understand

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:56.560
what a container image is.

00:12:56.560 --> 00:13:00.190
A container image is just
a directory of files,

00:13:00.190 --> 00:13:03.820
and in the docker image
format, this directory of files

00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:07.330
is split up into layers,
each represented as tarballs,

00:13:07.330 --> 00:13:09.970
and these tarballs composed
into the actual file

00:13:09.970 --> 00:13:12.220
system for the container.

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:13.660
And in this format,
there is also

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:15.929
a container
configuration, including

00:13:15.929 --> 00:13:17.470
things such as
environment variables,

00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:20.590
entry points, volume
amounts, exposed ports--

00:13:20.590 --> 00:13:23.000
things for when you're
actually running a container.

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:25.770
So this an example of a
container configuration.

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:28.800
You add the checksums for
layers, and an example--

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:30.610
there's also an entry
point set, which

00:13:30.610 --> 00:13:34.539
is a command that's run when
the container image is run.

00:13:34.539 --> 00:13:36.080
And then in this
format, there's also

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:38.570
a manifest that just wraps
up all the components

00:13:38.570 --> 00:13:39.980
of the container image--

00:13:39.980 --> 00:13:43.087
your layers and your
container configuration.

00:13:43.087 --> 00:13:44.420
This is an example of manifests.

00:13:44.420 --> 00:13:48.350
You have just IDs of the layers
and the configuration, as well.

00:13:48.350 --> 00:13:51.200
These are also checksums.

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:54.380
So in the Jib image, we
build our application layers

00:13:54.380 --> 00:13:57.290
on top of the
distroless/Java base image.

00:13:57.290 --> 00:14:00.560
Distroless is a set of
language focused base images

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:04.610
for languages such
as Java, Python, Go--

00:14:04.610 --> 00:14:06.650
and these images
don't include anything

00:14:06.650 --> 00:14:09.710
but just what is necessary
to run your application

00:14:09.710 --> 00:14:11.010
in that language.

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:13.710
So compare this to
Alpine, for example,

00:14:13.710 --> 00:14:17.080
which is a popular base
image recommended by docker.

00:14:17.080 --> 00:14:18.840
Alpine's still a
Linux distribution.

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:21.297
It's just a tiny
Linux distribution.

00:14:21.297 --> 00:14:22.880
Distroless, on the
other hand, doesn't

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:25.220
include package
managers, shells,

00:14:25.220 --> 00:14:26.990
or any other
unnecessary programs,

00:14:26.990 --> 00:14:28.920
but just what is necessary.

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:30.830
So this helps to
improve security

00:14:30.830 --> 00:14:32.996
by reducing possible
attack surfaces.

00:14:32.996 --> 00:14:34.370
It helps to keep
your containment

00:14:34.370 --> 00:14:36.212
image as minimal as possible.

00:14:36.212 --> 00:14:38.170
This is essentially what
a container should be.

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:40.400
It should be just
your application

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:42.810
and its runtime dependencies.

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:46.119
So if you guys want to
learn more about distroless,

00:14:46.119 --> 00:14:47.660
our colleague, Matt,
has a great talk

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:51.680
that's linked to in the
distroless repository.

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:53.190
APPU GOUNDAN: So speed.

00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:55.160
If we're trying to
replace your docker build,

00:14:55.160 --> 00:14:59.210
we want to be as fast or faster
than what you were previously

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:01.160
getting.

00:15:01.160 --> 00:15:03.380
So the way builds
work in docker is

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:04.630
you create a bunch of images.

00:15:04.630 --> 00:15:07.700
See, in this example, you have
a 100 megabyte, say, base layer

00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:09.800
and a 50 megabyte application.

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:12.780
When you push it to a registry,
it sends both layers over.

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:14.689
However, when you
make a change, it only

00:15:14.689 --> 00:15:15.980
sends the layer that's changed.

00:15:15.980 --> 00:15:18.710
So let's say we change
something in our application

00:15:18.710 --> 00:15:20.780
and we do a docker
build and push.

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:24.590
This layer is rebuilt
and pushes the registry.

00:15:24.590 --> 00:15:28.025
And even a possibly
better way to do it

00:15:28.025 --> 00:15:30.380
is to split your
application even more.

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:34.080
Let's say here, 49 and 1, and
if we'd done it correctly,

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:36.220
we're making all our
changes during development

00:15:36.220 --> 00:15:37.940
in this 1 megabyte layer.

00:15:37.940 --> 00:15:41.100
So our sends are quick and they
send very little over the wire.

00:15:43.650 --> 00:15:45.670
In Jib, we don't
use a docker file,

00:15:45.670 --> 00:15:47.930
but this is what we
think an optimized docker

00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:50.510
file might look like
for a Java application.

00:15:50.510 --> 00:15:52.510
You see it's split into
dependencies, resources,

00:15:52.510 --> 00:15:54.580
and classes, and then
we have an endpoint

00:15:54.580 --> 00:15:56.020
that ties it all together.

00:15:56.020 --> 00:15:58.720
But Jib doesn't
use a docker file.

00:15:58.720 --> 00:15:59.870
It still builds like this.

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:07.790
So here's a small
example of showing you

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:09.340
how you get speed improvements.

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:11.870
Your first build
using Jib or docker--

00:16:11.870 --> 00:16:14.870
this docker is a docker
file example that we

00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:17.390
found on a spring tutorial.

00:16:17.390 --> 00:16:19.760
It uses a single layer
for your application,

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:22.100
as opposed to three
layers, like we do in Jib.

00:16:22.100 --> 00:16:23.880
And your first
build is the same.

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:26.120
You have to send everything
over to the registry.

00:16:26.120 --> 00:16:28.720
If you rebuild without changes,
they're both pretty fast

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:31.220
because they just have to go
through the motions of deciding

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:32.450
nothing's changed.

00:16:32.450 --> 00:16:34.370
However, when you
rebuild with changes to,

00:16:34.370 --> 00:16:36.300
let's say, just
your class files,

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:39.304
Jib is only going to
resend your classes layer,

00:16:39.304 --> 00:16:40.720
whereas a traditional
docker build

00:16:40.720 --> 00:16:43.460
will send your whole
application over because that's

00:16:43.460 --> 00:16:45.390
what you told it to do.

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:48.670
And here, it's the difference
between 5 and, say, 15 seconds.

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:55.340
But if you look at a much larger
application down at the bottom,

00:16:55.340 --> 00:16:58.160
when you change, this one
might be heavy on dependencies

00:16:58.160 --> 00:16:59.477
and resources.

00:16:59.477 --> 00:17:01.060
So when you change
classes, again, Jib

00:17:01.060 --> 00:17:03.230
is only sending a
small layer over,

00:17:03.230 --> 00:17:05.300
while your traditional
docker build

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:06.980
is going to send the
whole thing over.

00:17:06.980 --> 00:17:09.450
And that, in this
case, takes 35 seconds,

00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:13.020
which is a lot of time to
be waiting for your build.

00:17:17.166 --> 00:17:18.540
QINGYANG CHEN: So
another benefit

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:20.520
we get out of a
custom image builder

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:24.829
like Jib is the ability to
build images reproducibly.

00:17:24.829 --> 00:17:27.109
So what does
reproducibility mean?

00:17:27.109 --> 00:17:30.380
Reproducibility means
that the same code always

00:17:30.380 --> 00:17:32.100
produces the same image.

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:34.760
So for example, if you
were to make a change

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:37.970
and commit that to, say,
your version control,

00:17:37.970 --> 00:17:41.150
that commit should produce
the same image on your machine

00:17:41.150 --> 00:17:44.570
as your teammate's machine, your
production machine, or your CIC

00:17:44.570 --> 00:17:45.810
system.

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:47.630
And what this helps
you do is produce

00:17:47.630 --> 00:17:51.260
possible variations that could
occur in a production container

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:54.860
build that you can't reproduce
in a local development build.

00:17:54.860 --> 00:17:57.110
So how do we achieve
reproducibility?

00:17:57.110 --> 00:17:59.090
Well, we just wipe
all the metadata

00:17:59.090 --> 00:18:02.510
that could vary between builds,
such as time stamps, user

00:18:02.510 --> 00:18:05.970
IDs, and groups.

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:09.152
So now that we've talked a
little bit about how Jib works,

00:18:09.152 --> 00:18:10.610
there's still much
more that we can

00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:15.329
do to improve the
containerization experience.

00:18:15.329 --> 00:18:16.870
APPU GOUNDAN: So
this is kind of what

00:18:16.870 --> 00:18:19.930
we're hoping to cover in
our next versions of Jib.

00:18:19.930 --> 00:18:21.384
We can have smarter
inferences, we

00:18:21.384 --> 00:18:23.800
can try and figure out what
port you need to expose and do

00:18:23.800 --> 00:18:26.659
that for you with these
other container optimizations

00:18:26.659 --> 00:18:29.200
based on your Java application,
like how much memory you need

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:31.690
or how much CPU you might need.

00:18:31.690 --> 00:18:34.270
We can try to organize
your layers logically,

00:18:34.270 --> 00:18:36.820
based on what code we think
is going to change together.

00:18:36.820 --> 00:18:39.260
So we can hopefully have
faster builds there.

00:18:39.260 --> 00:18:41.380
And then Java modules--

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:43.720
they let you reduce
the size of your JRE,

00:18:43.720 --> 00:18:46.360
and we could possibly reduce
the size of your container

00:18:46.360 --> 00:18:48.100
completely.

00:18:48.100 --> 00:18:50.200
So we've covered the build
part, but we're still

00:18:50.200 --> 00:18:52.780
trying to make it easier
for Java developers

00:18:52.780 --> 00:18:55.600
to get their code
running on a container.

00:18:55.600 --> 00:18:58.510
And our next demo is
an experimental plugin

00:18:58.510 --> 00:19:01.270
we're working on to help
during development that'll

00:19:01.270 --> 00:19:04.720
get your Jib built image
running and available

00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:06.600
for debugging on Kubernetes.

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:10.980
QINGYANG CHEN: So
now, we'll give

00:19:10.980 --> 00:19:14.520
a demo of the experimental
plugin we've been developing

00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:17.280
for being able to run
and debug your Jib

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:19.890
build container on Kubernetes.

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:21.540
So just a little bit of context.

00:19:21.540 --> 00:19:24.230
Kubernetes is a tool for
being able to orchestrate

00:19:24.230 --> 00:19:27.120
your containers across
a cluster of machines.

00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:29.280
And here, before
a demo, we already

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:32.850
set up a Kubernetes cluster
on Google Kubernetes engine.

00:19:32.850 --> 00:19:34.000
It's very easy to do.

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:36.431
All you need to do is
run that one command.

00:19:36.431 --> 00:19:37.680
So we already had that set up.

00:19:37.680 --> 00:19:41.860
We're going to go
into our terminal

00:19:41.860 --> 00:19:45.320
and we're going to stop this
container from last demo.

00:19:45.320 --> 00:19:48.520
And so kubecontrol is the
command line interface

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:51.430
for interacting with
your Kubernetes cluster.

00:19:51.430 --> 00:19:52.960
Can everyone see
this in the back?

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:53.540
Is it good?

00:19:53.540 --> 00:19:54.600
All right.

00:19:54.600 --> 00:19:58.870
So I'm just going to show that
we have an empty Kubernetes

00:19:58.870 --> 00:19:59.895
cluster setup.

00:19:59.895 --> 00:20:01.600
This might take a little while.

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:03.340
Every time, first
time you run it,

00:20:03.340 --> 00:20:05.469
it might be fishing
credentials-- something.

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:14.340
In the meantime-- so we're
going to go and apply

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:17.490
the experimental plugin
we've been developing.

00:20:17.490 --> 00:20:19.870
So I have this as a
live template, as well.

00:20:19.870 --> 00:20:23.610
So what I'm doing is
I'm applying this plugin

00:20:23.610 --> 00:20:24.840
without any configuration.

00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:27.660
It'll be able to just
use the Jib's image.

00:20:27.660 --> 00:20:31.397
So this is called the Kubernetes
dev Maven plugin for now.

00:20:31.397 --> 00:20:32.480
Let's go back to terminal.

00:20:32.480 --> 00:20:34.700
So you can see the
cluster is set up

00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:36.510
with just Kubernetes running.

00:20:36.510 --> 00:20:39.380
So now, what we can do is
we can take a project--

00:20:39.380 --> 00:20:43.010
or Maven project-- and
do Maven Kubernetes dev,

00:20:43.010 --> 00:20:44.930
and we'll do the
debug goal for being

00:20:44.930 --> 00:20:48.160
able to debug our application.

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:53.880
I'm going to expose
the Port 8080 again,

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:55.470
and what this is
going to do is it's

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:57.720
going to generate the
Kubernetes manifests.

00:20:57.720 --> 00:21:01.080
It's going to deploy those
manifests to the Kubernetes

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:04.910
cluster, and it's running the
application-- the container

00:21:04.910 --> 00:21:09.090
and the application with
the debugger agent library

00:21:09.090 --> 00:21:12.240
and exposing that port and
actually forwarding that port

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:16.300
to our computer, so that
we can debug it in our IDE.

00:21:16.300 --> 00:21:20.100
So what we can do now is
we can go back to our IDE

00:21:20.100 --> 00:21:22.032
and go into--

00:21:22.032 --> 00:21:23.490
it might be a little
harder to see,

00:21:23.490 --> 00:21:27.840
but Run, Edit Configurations,
and all we're going to do

00:21:27.840 --> 00:21:31.320
is we're going to add a default
remote bugging configuration.

00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:36.700
So add a default remote, and
we're just going to press OK.

00:21:36.700 --> 00:21:39.400
And now what we can
do is we can go to Run

00:21:39.400 --> 00:21:44.150
and just debug that
configuration we just made.

00:21:44.150 --> 00:21:46.660
And now our IDE's
debugger is connected

00:21:46.660 --> 00:21:49.840
to our application that's
running in our Kubernetes

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:51.750
cluster.

00:21:51.750 --> 00:21:55.270
So as you can see, the
application is starting up,

00:21:55.270 --> 00:21:57.290
and once this starts
up, we can actually

00:21:57.290 --> 00:21:59.570
hit it from our web browser.

00:21:59.570 --> 00:22:01.900
But first, let's see
what is in the cluster.

00:22:06.160 --> 00:22:08.670
So as you can see, the
pet clinic application

00:22:08.670 --> 00:22:11.650
has started up as a
service in the cluster,

00:22:11.650 --> 00:22:13.710
and it's generated
an external IP

00:22:13.710 --> 00:22:15.790
that we can actually hit live.

00:22:18.780 --> 00:22:23.060
Anyone can actually
hit it in this room.

00:22:23.060 --> 00:22:26.205
So we can go there.

00:22:26.205 --> 00:22:27.670
Let's first see if
it's started up.

00:22:27.670 --> 00:22:28.580
It is started up.

00:22:28.580 --> 00:22:29.396
OK.

00:22:29.396 --> 00:22:30.520
So let me make this bigger.

00:22:30.520 --> 00:22:34.540
So as you can see, it's running
on our Kubernetes cluster,

00:22:34.540 --> 00:22:37.630
just like it was running
locally in our container.

00:22:37.630 --> 00:22:40.150
So this is in a
container on Kubernetes.

00:22:40.150 --> 00:22:42.100
So let's say we want
to do some debugging.

00:22:42.100 --> 00:22:46.360
We want to, let's say, just
don't show any veterinarians.

00:22:46.360 --> 00:22:49.870
So what we can do is we
can perform a hot swap.

00:22:49.870 --> 00:22:53.540
So we can go back
to IDE, and let's

00:22:53.540 --> 00:22:56.620
go to the controller
for that page--

00:22:56.620 --> 00:22:58.642
the /vets.html.

00:22:58.642 --> 00:23:00.890
And let's just
comment out the line

00:23:00.890 --> 00:23:03.240
that just adds all the
veterinarians to the page.

00:23:03.240 --> 00:23:08.020
You can save that, go
to build, recompile.

00:23:08.020 --> 00:23:09.990
So this will recompile
the class and it'll

00:23:09.990 --> 00:23:12.010
ask us to do the hot spot.

00:23:12.010 --> 00:23:13.770
So asks if it's a real class.

00:23:13.770 --> 00:23:14.270
Yes.

00:23:14.270 --> 00:23:17.740
We'll reload it, and this
will swap out the class

00:23:17.740 --> 00:23:20.240
that's running on Kubernetes.

00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:23.030
So now, if you go back
to our page and refresh,

00:23:23.030 --> 00:23:26.340
we'll see that there's
no veterinarians left.

00:23:26.340 --> 00:23:27.260
Cool.

00:23:27.260 --> 00:23:29.200
Let's go back to
the presentation.

00:23:33.560 --> 00:23:34.060
All right.

00:23:34.060 --> 00:23:36.610
So that was a lot of
stuff we just did,

00:23:36.610 --> 00:23:38.350
but what did we just do?

00:23:38.350 --> 00:23:42.160
We took our project, we
ran some Maven plugin.

00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:43.600
A goal was a Maven plugin.

00:23:43.600 --> 00:23:46.670
And it put our application
in containerized form

00:23:46.670 --> 00:23:49.240
on Kubernetes for us
to be able to bug.

00:23:49.240 --> 00:23:53.120
And we were able to
use our IDE to attach

00:23:53.120 --> 00:23:57.310
to our running application and
debug our application that's

00:23:57.310 --> 00:23:59.650
running in remote
cluster as if it was

00:23:59.650 --> 00:24:02.020
a locally running application.

00:24:05.170 --> 00:24:06.810
APPU GOUNDAN: So
you saw the tools

00:24:06.810 --> 00:24:09.870
we built for helping you build--

00:24:09.870 --> 00:24:12.730
stuff we're working on to
help your run and deploy.

00:24:12.730 --> 00:24:16.220
The future now is
we're trying to make

00:24:16.220 --> 00:24:19.249
Jib available as a library
instead of just plugins.

00:24:19.249 --> 00:24:21.540
So if you're building your
own container builder tools,

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:23.580
you can use the
library underneath

00:24:23.580 --> 00:24:26.130
and have a lot of it
taken care of for you.

00:24:26.130 --> 00:24:31.347
We want to release the debug
and run plugin, possibly

00:24:31.347 --> 00:24:32.430
using Scaffold underneath.

00:24:32.430 --> 00:24:34.371
If any of you are
familiar with that,

00:24:34.371 --> 00:24:36.370
that's another team at
Google that we work with.

00:24:36.370 --> 00:24:38.855
And finally-- we
know this is hard,

00:24:38.855 --> 00:24:40.230
but we want Java
developers to be

00:24:40.230 --> 00:24:45.000
able to run their Java
applications on a container

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:51.550
cluster without having to know
anything about containers.

00:24:51.550 --> 00:24:53.650
QINGYANG CHEN: So Jib
is now available in beta

00:24:53.650 --> 00:24:57.530
and you can find it at
our GitHub repository.

00:24:57.530 --> 00:24:59.890
And we welcome you to check
it out, tweet about us,

00:24:59.890 --> 00:25:03.220
blog about us, and
leave us feedback.

00:25:03.220 --> 00:25:07.872
And we have quite a few
minutes for questions.

00:25:07.872 --> 00:25:09.580
So let us know if you
have any questions.

00:25:09.580 --> 00:25:12.520
We'll also probably be at
the Google booth afterwards.

00:25:12.520 --> 00:25:13.589
Thanks a lot.

00:25:13.589 --> 00:25:15.585
